# Step 1: Create the Project

Create a new folder on VSCode by entering into the terminal:
mkdir terraform-cloud-init
cd terraform-cloud-init


# Step 2: Create files

Add the following files by using the touch command on the terminal and then entering:
main.tf
variables.tf
outputs.tf
provider.tf
terraform.tfvars
cloud-init.yaml


# Step 3: provider.tf

On provider.tf, enter in the following:

terraform {
  required_version = ">= 1.5"

  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "~> 6.0"
    }
  }
}

provider "aws" {
  region = var.region
}


# Step 4: variables.tf

On variables.tf, enter in the following:

variable "region" {
  default = "eu-west-2"
}

variable "instance_type" {
  default = "t3.micro"
}

variable "key_name" {
  description = "AWS Key Pair"
}

Change the region to match your aws console.


# Step 5: terraform.tfvars

Add your key pair onto this folder.
key_name = "YOUR-KEY-NAME"


# Step 6: Ubuntu AMI

Find your latest Ubuntu AMI with the following:
aws ssm get-parameters \
--names /aws/service/canonical/ubuntu/server/24.04/stable/current/amd64/hvm/ebs-gp3/ami-id \
--region eu-west-2
Change to your specified region.
Copy the AMI ID that appears.


# Step 7: cloud-init.yaml

Enter the following onto this file:
#cloud-config

package_update: true
package_upgrade: true

packages:
  - nginx

runcmd:
  - systemctl enable nginx
  - systemctl start nginx
  - echo "<h1>Provisioned by Terraform + Cloud-Init</h1>" > /var/www/html/index.html

This should update Ubuntu, Install NGINX, Enable after reboot, Create a custom web page.
No need to SSH.


# Step 8: main.tf

Enter the following into your main.tf file to create the security group:

resource "aws_security_group" "web" {

  name = "cloud-init-web"

  ingress {
    from_port   = 80
    to_port     = 80
    protocol    = "tcp"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }

  ingress {
    from_port   = 22
    to_port     = 22
    protocol    = "tcp"
    cidr_blocks = ["YOUR_IP/32"]
  }

  egress {
    from_port = 0
    to_port   = 0
    protocol  = "-1"

    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }
}

Replace YOUR_IP with your public IP address.

Enter the following to create the EC2 instance:

resource "aws_instance" "web" {

  ami           = "YOUR_AMI"
  instance_type = var.instance_type
  key_name      = var.key_name

  vpc_security_group_ids = [
    aws_security_group.web.id
  ]

  user_data = file("${path.module}/cloud-init.yaml")

  tags = {
    Name = "Terraform-CloudInit"
  }
}


# Step 9: outputs.tf

Enter the following into the outputs.tf file:

output "instance_public_ip" {
  value = aws_instance.web.public_ip
}

output "website" {
  value = "http://${aws_instance.web.public_ip}"
}


# Step 10: Initialise Terraform

On the terminal, run: terraform init


# Step 11: Validate

Run: terraform validate


# Step 12: Review your terraform plan

Run: terraform plan
You should be able to see the security group and the EC2 instance.


# Step 13: Deployment

Deploy what you have created by running: terraform apply
This should create the security group, the EC2 and then pass cloud-init onto the EC2.
May take a few minutes as there is quite a few things to install.


# Step 14: Test

Copy your output http://
Open it on your browser and it should show: Provisioned by Terraform + Cloud-Init


# Step 15: Verify Cloud-init

SSH into the server with: ssh -i Wordpress-Terraform.pem ubuntu@PUBLIC_IP

Check the cloud-init log: sudo cat /var/log/cloud-init-output.log

Here you should be able to see the installation steps and any commands.


# Step 16: Destroy

Once you have gathered evidence of the project through notes and screenshots, you can destroy it to avoid incurring any AWS costs.
To do this, run: terraform destroy
This should destroy both the EC2 and the Security group.

